> phi 1
phi(1) = 1
  how: 1 = 1; phi = product of p^(e-1) (p - 1): 1 (the empty product)
  the order of the unit group (Z/1)*; lambda(1) = 1 is its exponent
  check: counted the k in 1..1 with gcd(k, 1) = 1: 1
> phi 12
phi(12) = 4
  how: 12 = 2^2 * 3; phi = product of p^(e-1) (p - 1): 2^1*1 * 2
  the order of the unit group (Z/12)*; lambda(12) = 2 is its exponent
  check: counted the k in 1..12 with gcd(k, 12) = 1: 4
> carmichael 561
lambda(561) = 80 (Carmichael: the least m with a^m = 1 for every unit a)
  how: 561 = 3 * 11 * 17; lambda is the lcm of 2 (3^1), 10 (11^1), 16 (17^1)
  lambda(2^e) = 2^(e-2) for e >= 3; lambda(p^e) = phi(p^e) otherwise; phi(561) = 320
  check: the lcm of the orders of all units, by stepping through their powers: 80
> legendre 2 7
(2/7) = 1
  a quadratic residue: x^2 = a has a solution
  (4^2 = 2 mod 7)
  how: quadratic reciprocity (the binary Jacobi algorithm)
  check: Euler's criterion 2^((7-1)/2) = 1 mod 7
> jacobi 2 15
(2/15) = 1
  how: quadratic reciprocity; the product of the Legendre symbols over 15 = 3 * 5
  15 is composite: (a/n) = 1 does not make a a square mod n; here 2 is not a square mod 15
> sqrtmod 2 7
x^2 = 2 mod 7: x = 3, 4
  how: p = 3 mod 4: x = a^((p+1)/4)
  check: 3^2 = 2, 4^2 = 2 (mod 7); brute force over 0..6: 2 solutions
> sqrtmod 1 16
x^2 = 1 mod 16: x = 1, 7, 9, 15
  how: 16 = 2^4: roots mod each prime (Tonelli-Shanks), lifted to the prime powers (Hensel), combined by CRT
  check: 1^2 = 1, 7^2 = 1, 9^2 = 1, 15^2 = 1 (mod 16); brute force over 0..15: 4 solutions
> inverse 3 mod 11
3^-1 = 4 mod 11
  how: extended Euclid: 4*3 - 1*11 = 1
  check: 3 * 4 = 12 = 1*11 + 1
> order 3 mod 7
ord(3) = 6 mod 7
  how: the order divides lambda(7) = 6; 3^6 = 1 and 3^(6/2) = 6, 3^(6/3) = 2
  3 is a primitive root mod 7 (its order is phi(7) = 6)
  additive order: 7
  check: stepping through the powers, 3^k = 1 first at k = 6
> crt 2 mod 3, 3 mod 5
the system
  2 mod 3: x = 2
    x = 2 (mod 3)
  3 mod 5: x = 3
    x = 3 (mod 5)
x = 8 (mod 15)
  how: the Chinese remainder theorem, pairwise (moduli that share factors must agree mod their gcd)
  check: the solution substituted into every congruence: it holds; brute force over 0..14: 1 solution
> ring Z/12 zerodivisors
  zero divisors: 7 nonzero (every nonzero non-unit of a finite ring): 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 10
> factorint 561
561 = 3 * 11 * 17 (not prime)
  a Carmichael number: a^(n-1) = 1 for every a coprime to it (lambda(561) = 80 divides n - 1), so Fermat's test is fooled
  how: trial division by the primes below 50, then Pollard-Brent rho; primality by Miller-Rabin with the first 12 prime bases (deterministic below 2^64)
  check: the product is 561; each factor is prime
